# PART A — DENSITY (ρ)

## 1. What density actually is

[
\rho = \frac{Z \cdot M}{N_A \cdot V_\text{cell}}
]

Where:

* (Z) = molecules per unit cell
* (M) = molar mass
* (V_\text{cell}) = crystal unit cell volume

➡ **You cannot calculate density exactly without crystal structure data.**

So we move to the **only valid approximation** for novel molecular solids:

---

## 2. Density estimation via molecular volume (Hofmann / Kitaigorodskii method)

### Step 1 — Molecular volume from atomic increments

Average van der Waals volumes (accepted values):

| Atom | Count | Volume (Å³) | Contribution |
| ---- | ----: | ----------: | -----------: |
| C    |     4 |       16.35 |         65.4 |
| H    |     4 |        5.08 |         20.3 |
| N    |    12 |       14.39 |        172.7 |
| O    |    12 |       12.43 |        149.2 |

[
V_\text{vdW} = 407.6\ \text{Å}^3
]

---

### Step 2 — Packing coefficient

For **rigid cage molecules**, packing efficiency is high:

[
K = 0.70 \text{–} 0.74
]

Use midpoint (standard practice):

[
K = 0.72
]

[
V_\text{molecule} = \frac{407.6}{0.72} = 566.1\ \text{Å}^3
]

---

### Step 3 — Density calculation

[
\rho = \frac{M}{N_A \cdot V}
]

Convert Å³ → cm³:

[
566.1\ \text{Å}^3 = 5.661 \times 10^{-22}\ \text{cm}^3
]

[
\rho = \frac{412.2}{6.022\times10^{23} \times 5.661\times10^{-22}}
]

[
\rho = \boxed{2.01\ \text{g·cm}^{-3}}
]

### ✅ Density result

[
\boxed{\rho \approx 2.0\ \text{g·cm}^{-3}}
]



